 # Hardware-Assisted Projection
-
 ## Overview
-Hardware-Assisted Projection is a family of volume rendering techniques that offload projection, scan conversion, and compositing to the graphics processing unit (GPU) [G]. It addresses the performance bottlenecks of CPU-based projected tetrahedra by leveraging parallel hardware architecture to achieve interactive rendering rates [G]. Unlike traditional raycasting, which operates in image space, these methods preserve the object-order nature of projected tetrahedra while utilizing hardware pipelines for rasterization and fragment processing, often removing the need for explicit CPU-side sorting or triangle generation [G].
-
+Hardware-Assisted Projection is a family of volume rendering techniques for unstructured grids that project 3D cells (tetrahedra or polyhedra) into screen space to leverage GPU rasterization pipelines, thereby avoiding the high data transfer overhead of CPU-side triangle generation and sorting required by traditional [Projected Tetrahedra](877-projected-tetrahedra.md). It differs from sibling methods by offloading the geometric decomposition, scan conversion, or integral evaluation to hardware shaders, enabling single-pass or reduced-pass rendering [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
 ## Shared principles
-All methods in this family map 3D unstructured grid elements (tetrahedra or polyhedra) to 2D screen space using the graphics pipeline [G]. The general workflow involves:
-1. **Projection:** Transforming 3D vertices to screen coordinates using view and projection matrices [G].
-2. **Scan Conversion:** Rasterizing the projected elements into fragments, which may involve generating triangles or processing point primitives decomposed by a geometry shader [G].
-3. **Volume Integration:** Computing the volume ray integral within the fragment shader, often using partial pre-integration techniques to handle opacity and color accumulation accurately [G].
-4. **Compositing:** Accumulating the color and opacity of fragments, typically using front-to-back alpha blending [G].
-Key shared characteristics include a GPU-centric pipeline that minimizes CPU-GPU data transfer [G], minimal explicit pre-processing beyond loading mesh data into GPU memory [G], and depth sorting requirements for accurate compositing, achieved via pre-sorting or hardware depth buffering [G].
-
+The family shares the core mechanism of object-order projection: mapping 3D cell vertices to 2D screen coordinates to determine the projection area, followed by scan conversion to identify ray-cell intersections. Unlike pure raycasting, these methods operate in image space, generating depth-sorted ray segments or fragment-level contributions that are composited using hardware acceleration. The primary goal is to minimize CPU-GPU data transfer and eliminate explicit CPU-side triangle sorting by utilizing GPU geometry shaders, fragment shaders, or hardware compositing [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Hardware-Assisted Polyhedron Projection](1453-hardware-assisted-polyhedron-projection.md)
-Projects arbitrary convex polyhedral cells to 2D screen space, performs scan conversion to generate depth-sorted ray segments, and composites them [G]. Differs from tetrahedral methods by handling general polyhedra and relying on global sorting for depth ordering [G]. Choose when dealing with unstructured grids containing non-tetrahedral cells [G].
+Projects arbitrary convex polyhedral cells to 2D, performs scan conversion to generate depth-sorted ray segments, and composites them using hardware acceleration. It differs from raycasting by operating in image space with object-order projection, allowing flexible data distribution on distributed-memory systems and leveraging standard polygon pipelines for scan conversion. Unlike [Projected Tetrahedra](877-projected-tetrahedra.md), which requires explicit triangle generation and sorting, this method projects cells directly as polygons and relies on hardware compositing or global sorting for depth ordering.
 
 ### [Hardware-Assisted Projected Tetrahedra](1729-hardware-assisted-projected-tetrahedra.md)
-Streams sorted tetrahedra as point primitives and uses a geometry shader to decompose them into triangles, evaluating the volume ray integral per fragment [G]. Differs from standard PT by eliminating CPU-side sorting and triangle generation overhead [G]. Choose for interactive rendering of large tetrahedral volumes exceeding GPU memory limits [G].
+Projects tetrahedra onto the screen and rasterizes them as triangles, evaluating the volume ray integral per fragment in the fragment shader. It solves the performance bottleneck of explicit isosurface extraction and the data transfer overhead of previous hybrid methods by performing the entire rendering pipeline on the GPU in a single pass. Unlike its sibling [Projected Tetrahedra](877-projected-tetrahedra.md), which requires CPU-side sorting and triangle generation, HAPT streams sorted tetrahedra as point primitives and uses the GPU geometry shader to decompose them into triangles. It differs from [Tetrahedral Projection with Partial Pre-integration](1861-tetrahedral-projection-with-partial-pre-integration.md) (PTINT) by eliminating the need to read data back from the GPU, allowing rendering of volumes with several million tetrahedra that would exceed GPU memory limits.
 
-### [GPU-Based Projected Tetrahedra](1951-gpu-based-projected-tetrahedra.md)
-Implements the standard PT algorithm using programmable graphics hardware with a two-fragment-shader architecture [G]. Differs from siblings by focusing on direct GPU-based projection and rasterization without redundant pre-processing [G]. Choose for interactive visualization of unstructured tetrahedral data with a smaller memory footprint than raycasting [G].
+### Röttger 3D Texture Mapping
+An extension of the projected tetrahedra algorithm for hardware-accelerated rendering of volumetric data using 3D texture mapping [S1, unofficial]. Linearly approximate parts of the volume integral depending on segment length by modulating vertex colors. Tabulate remaining parts that depend only on textural coordinates in a 2D texture map [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The family builds on [Projected Tetrahedra](877-projected-tetrahedra.md), adapting the CPU-based algorithm to leverage GPU hardware acceleration [G]. Key developments include the use of geometry shaders for dynamic triangle generation and the application of partial pre-integration in fragment shaders for accurate volume rendering [G]. The methods are widely used in interactive visualization of unstructured grids, such as in finite element analysis and medical imaging, where large datasets require real-time feedback [G].
-
-## Sources+The concept of hardware-assisted projection for unstructured grids emerged as a response to the limitations of CPU-based projected tetrahedra methods, which suffered from high data transfer costs and sorting bottlenecks. Early work by Röttger et al. extended projected tetrahedra for hardware-accelerated rendering using 3D texture mapping [S1, unofficial]. Later developments, such as Hardware-Assisted Projected Tetrahedra (HAPT), leveraged GPU geometry shaders to decompose tetrahedra into triangles on-the-fly, enabling single-pass rendering without CPU-side triangle generation [S2, unofficial]. Hardware-Assisted Polyhedron Projection generalized this approach to arbitrary convex polyhedra, supporting distributed-memory systems and flexible data distribution [S2][S3, unofficial].
